WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.380
- The United States' Declaration
of Independence reads,

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:07.030
we hold these truths to be self-evident

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.982
that all men are created equal,

00:00:09.982 --> 00:00:12.470
that they are endowed by their creator

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:15.720
with certain unalienable rights.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.590
That sounds great, but
who does it apply to?

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.100
And what are those rights?

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.100
That's what we are going to
discuss in these lessons.

00:00:26.547 --> 00:00:28.200
We're going to think about what rights

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.890
do you have as an individual,

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:33.260
and what rights might
you give up to the state.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.980
We'll talk about the
history of civil rights

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.890
and civil liberties in the United States.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.290
Even though we have these very
powerful and very high-minded

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.110
words in the Declaration
of Independence in 1776,

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:48.280
not all men, and especially not all women,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.200
had these types of rights,

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.683
as the nation was founded
in the 1780s and 1790s.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.000
How did that evolve,
and how will that story

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.330
continue to evolve as the United States

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.680
tries to become a more perfect union?

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.210
In these lessons, we're gonna make sure

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.670
we understand the difference between, say,

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.210
a civil liberty and a civil right.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.150
We're also going to learn a
lot about the Bill of Rights

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.830
and what liberties and
rights they protect.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:21.310
And finally, we're going to
learn how people have organized

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.470
throughout American history to make change

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.100
and to ensure that as a nation,

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.960
we are as just of a nation as possible.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.620
And so whether your concern is

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.470
what rights do I have if I get arrested

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:39.920
to how do I make sure that
I live in a just society,

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.640
these lessons are going
to be interesting for you.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.040
So I'm excited about going
on this journey with you.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.940
And if you want to learn more

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.020
and get deep practice
with immediate feedback,

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:53.933
I encourage you to look at these
lessons on KhanAcademy.org.

